{
  "raw": {
    "https://facultypositions.stanford.edu/jobs/classics-and-civics-senior-rank-faculty-position-in-classics-stanford-university-california-united-states": {
      "title": "Classics and Civics, Senior Rank Faculty Position in Classics",
      "description": "Classics and Civics, Senior Rank Faculty Position in Classics - Stanford University, California, United States Stanford Faculty Positions Site navigation Applicant Login My Profile Kiosk mode Open Faculty Positions Classics and Civics, Senior Rank Faculty Position in Classics Stanford University University Tenure Line Opening at: Jul 30 2026 - 10:15am PDT Closing at: Nov 6 2026 - 11:55pm PST Classics and Civics, Senior Rank Faculty Position in Classics The Department of Classics at Stanford University seeks applicants for a senior appointment at the rank of Associate Professor or Professor. We are looking for a senior scholar (a tenured Associate or Full Professor) whose research profile aligns with key areas of the Stanford Civics Initiative; we are especially interested in scholars of Roman rhetoric and Roman political philosophy. Applicants must have demonstrated a commitment to effective teaching and mentoring and the ability to maintain a world-class research program. The successful candidate must have a Ph.D. in a related field and will be expected to teach and advise students at both the graduate and undergraduate levels. The term of appointment is expected to begin on September 1, 2027. While successful applicants will be home-based in a Stanford Department (Classics, History, Philosophy, or Political Science), teaching and service responsibilities will be divided equally between the home department and the Stanford Civics Initiative , an interdisciplinary program offering a Minor in Civic Thought and Practice. We seek applicants whose teaching and scholarship will support one or more of the four CTP Minor tracks (Civic History, Civic Thought, Constitution and Law, Political Economy) and whose approach to pedagogy is aligned with the Statement of Principles of the Alliance for Civics in the Academy:  \"Preparing students with civic skills, knowledge, and experience relevant to understanding and exercising the rights and duties of citizenship in a self-governing republic is a basic responsibility of American colleges and universities. Faculty must intentionally engage with historically significant competing arguments on contested questions of civic life, oppose indoctrination, and model the democratic values of open inquiry and freedom of expression. Civic education should be a shared intellectual activity, grounded in texts, aimed at building a practical capacity for listening to and acting together with others, including those of differing beliefs.” Successful applicants will also teach one course yearly in Stanford’s COLLEGE program for first-year students. Candidates should apply through Stanford Faculty Positions and submit a cover letter curriculum vitae (including a publication list) a research and teaching statement (no more than three single-spaced pages), including a description of past research and an outline of future research plans The Classics Department, School of Humanities & Sciences, and Stanford University value faculty who will help foster an open and respectful academic environment for colleagues, students, and staff with a wide range of backgrounds and perspectives. Candidates may choose to include as part of their research and teaching statements a brief discussion about how their work and experience will further these values. The salary range for this position for the academic year 2026-2027 is as follows: Associate Professor: $128,992 - $159,907 Professor: $149,247 - $255,852 Stanford University has provided a pay range representing its good faith estimate of what the university reasonably expects to pay for the position upon hire. The pay offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors including (but not limited to) the qualifications of the selected candidate, budget availability, and internal equity. Further information about Stanford’s Department of Classics may be found at classics.stanford.edu. Inquiries may be directed to Claudia Ortega, Director of Finance and Administration, ciortega@stanford.edu. Review of applications will begin on November 3; later applications may be considered at the discretion of the search committee. The application process will close on November 2, 2026. Stanford is an equal employment opportunity and affirmative action employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law. Stanford welcomes applications from all who would bring additional dimensions to the University’s research, teaching and clinical missions. Consistent with its obligations under the law, the University will provide reasonable accommodations to applicants and employees with disabilities. Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application or hiring process should contact disability.access@stanford.edu. Back to all jobs Get Help Questions about a job or your application? Find out how to request help Equal Opportunity Stanford is an equal employment opportunity and affirmative action employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law. Stanford welcomes applications from all who would bring additional dimensions to the University’s research, teaching and clinical missions. Consistent with its obligations under the law, the University will provide reasonable accommodations to applicants and employees with disabilities. Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application or hiring process should contact disability.access@stanford.edu . Stanford University Stanford Home Maps & Directions Search Stanford Emergency Info Terms of Use Cookie Preferences Privacy Copyright Trademarks Non-Discrimination Accessibility © 2026 Stanford University. Stanford, California 94305. Candidate Details We use cookies Some are necessary to operate the website and its functions. Others help personalise, improve content and services to show you the most relevant job opportunities. With the decision \"Accept essential only\" we will respect your privacy and will not set cookies that aren't necessary for the operation of the site. Accept essential only Accept all"
    },
    "https://facultypositions.stanford.edu/jobs/assistant-associate-or-full-professor-of-health-policy-stanford-university-california-united-states": {
      "title": "Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor of Health Policy",
      "description": "Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor of Health Policy - Stanford University, California, United States Stanford Faculty Positions Site navigation Applicant Login My Profile Kiosk mode Open Faculty Positions Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor of Health Policy Stanford University University Medical Line University Tenure Line Opening at: Oct 29 2026 - 6:00am PDT Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor of Health Policy Department of Health Policy Stanford University School of Medicine The Department of Health Policy at Stanford University seeks an Assistant Professor, Associate Professor, or Professor in the University Tenure Line or University Medical Line.  We are particularly interested in candidates with expertise in health policy/health services research with a special emphasis on health and healthcare disparities. The predominant criterion for appointment in the University Tenure Line is a major commitment to research and teaching. The major criteria for appointment for faculty in the University Medical Line shall be excellence in the overall mix of clinical care, clinical teaching, scholarly activity that advances clinical medicine, and institutional service appropriate to the programmatic need the individual is expected to fulfill. Faculty rank and line will be determined by the qualifications and experience of the successful candidate. The Department of Health Policy (DHP) is a multidisciplinary group with broad research and education programs in health policy, both domestic and global (https://healthpolicy.fsi.stanford.edu/).  DHP is closely affiliated with Stanford’s Center for Health Policy (CHP) in the Freeman Spogli Institute for International Studies and works with multiple departments and Schools throughout the university. Applicants should have a Ph.D. degree or an M.D. with substantial research background and/or training in research. Training and expertise should be in health policy with a focus on improving population health from a variety of disciplines (e.g., health services research, decision sciences, health economics, data science). We are particularly interested in individuals whose portfolio includes understanding and addressing disparities in health and healthcare. Specific areas of emphasis may include, but are not limited to, understanding policy level drivers of disparities in health outcomes, understanding systematic differences in the diffusion or impact of healthcare technology, evaluating policies to improve access to care, understanding the impact of the economics and organization of care on healthcare access, quality and safety for subgroups at high risk for adverse outcomes, and informing the design of programs and policies to reduce disparities in care and outcomes. The successful candidate is expected to develop an independent research program and to participate in the School of Medicine’s training and teaching programs in health policy. Stanford is an equal employment opportunity and affirmative action employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law. Stanford welcomes applications from all who would bring additional dimensions to the University’s research, teaching and clinical missions. Consistent with its obligations under the law, the University will provide reasonable accommodations to applicants and employees with disabilities. Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application or hiring process should contact disability.access@stanford.edu . The university’s central functions of research and education depend on freedom of thought, and expression. The Department of Health Policy, School of Medicine, and Stanford University value faculty who will help foster an open and respectful academic environment for colleagues, students, and staff with a wide range of backgrounds, identities, and perspectives. Candidates may choose to include as part of their research and teaching statements a brief discussion about how their work and experience will further these values. To apply, please submit a curriculum vitae, one research paper (published or unpublished), and a letter addressed to the Search Committee Chair describing your research interests and teaching experience. Early career applicants should also include the names and email addresses of three references.  References will not be contacted without the candidate’s prior knowledge. All materials should be submitted using the Apply Now button above. Applications will be reviewed beginning in November 2026 and should be received no later than January 6, 2027 to receive full consideration. For questions, please contact bsc-academicaffairs@lists.stanford.edu . Please note that Stanford University will be closed for winter break from December 21, 2026, through January 3, 2027 and staff will be unavailable to respond to inquiries during this period. Responses will resume on the morning of January 4, 2027. Applicants are encouraged to submit questions before the closure or promptly after the university reopens. The expected base pay range for this position is: Assistant Professor: $254,000 - $292,000 Associate Professor: $340,000 - $359,000 Professor: $418,000 - $445,000 This pay range reflects base pay, which is based on faculty rank and years in rank. It does not include all components of the School of Medicine’s faculty compensation program or pay from participation in departmental incentive compensation programs. For more information about compensation and our wide-range of benefits , including housing assistance , please contact the hiring department. Stanford University has provided a pay range representing its good faith estimate of what the university reasonably expects to pay for the position upon hire. The pay offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors including (but not limited to) the experience and qualifications of the selected candidate including equivalent years in rank, training, and field or discipline; internal equity; and external market pay for comparable jobs. Back to all jobs Get Help Questions about a job or your application? Find out how to request help Equal Opportunity Stanford is an equal employment opportunity and affirmative action employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law. Stanford welcomes applications from all who would bring additional dimensions to the University’s research, teaching and clinical missions. Consistent with its obligations under the law, the University will provide reasonable accommodations to applicants and employees with disabilities. Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application or hiring process should contact disability.access@stanford.edu . Stanford University Stanford Home Maps & Directions Search Stanford Emergency Info Terms of Use Cookie Preferences Privacy Copyright Trademarks Non-Discrimination Accessibility © 2026 Stanford University. Stanford, California 94305. Candidate Details We use cookies Some are necessary to operate the website and its functions. Others help personalise, improve content and services to show you the most relevant job opportunities. With the decision \"Accept essential only\" we will respect your privacy and will not set cookies that aren't necessary for the operation of the site. Accept essential only Accept all"
    },
    "https://facultypositions.stanford.edu/jobs/associate-chief-medical-officer-for-managed-care-stanford-university-california-united-states": {
      "title": "Associate Chief Medical Officer for Managed Care",
      "description": "Associate Chief Medical Officer for Managed Care - Stanford University, California, United States Stanford Faculty Positions Site navigation Applicant Login My Profile Kiosk mode Open Faculty Positions Associate Chief Medical Officer for Managed Care Stanford University CE - Clinician Educator Opening at: Oct 28 2026 - 9:00am PDT Stanford University School of Medicine seeks an Associate Chief Medical Officer (ACMO) for Managed Care, with an academic appointment in the Clinician Educator line. The primary criterion for appointment is excellence in a combination of clinical care, teaching, administration, and/or scholarship aligned with the programmatic needs the individual is expected to fulfill. The ACMO is a physician executive responsible for leading Stanford Medicine Children’s Health’s payer strategy, reimbursement, and access to destination services. The ACMO partners with clinical, operational, and executive leaders to optimize payer relationships, support utilization strategies, improve access to specialized pediatric services, and advance organizational goals related to financial performance and patient experience. The ACMO serves as a key physician leader for complex payer, authorization, medical necessity, and reimbursement matters; provides clinical oversight and consultation across revenue cycle, utilization management, and care coordination; and fosters physician engagement through education and guidance on best practices to mitigate denials and safeguard reimbursement. As a member of the Senior Leadership Team, the ACMO provides leadership for strategic programs and partnerships that support Stanford Children's mission and long-term growth. Essential Functions Payer Strategy and Contracting Provide clinical leadership for payer contracts and denial management strategies. Cultivate relationships with health plan clinical and administrative leaders, including medical directors and executives. Partner with payers and SMCH stakeholders to resolve complex coverage, reimbursement, and access issues, including facilitation of Letters of Agreement (LOAs) and single-case agreements. Collaborate with internal and external stakeholders to improve patient access to specialized pediatric services. Assist with analysis of reimbursement performance across all payers to identify and prioritize improvement opportunities. Clinical Oversight for Revenue Cycle, Utilization and Care Coordination Provide physician consultation and oversight for complex utilization management, medical necessity, and care coordination issues. Serve as executive escalation point and provide physician consultation on complex financial clearance, authorization, reimbursement, and denial issues for both in-network and out-of-network health plans (including out-of-state Medicaid plans.) Partner with Revenue Cycle, Managed Care, Case Management, and Utilization Review teams to address payer challenges and improve reimbursement outcomes. Identify denial trends and opportunities to improve access to care, financial clearance, and reimbursement. Oversee the Stanford Children’s International Patient Services (SCIPS) program for assisting international families seeking elective care at SMCH. Provide oversight to a team of assigned medical directors working in Case Management, UM, CDI, HIMS, financial clearance and other medical directors as assigned. Physician Education and Clinical Consultation In partnership with Revenue Cycle Operations, serve as the physician resource for questions, issues, and escalations regarding regulatory and payer requirements for coverage of medically necessary services, financial clearance, and reimbursement. Educate and coach physicians on documentation, authorization requirements, and payer expectations / guidelines. Work with operational and clinical leaders to develop guidance and best practices to improve authorization approvals and reduce administrative barriers to care. Provide guidance to physicians on complex payer, utilization, and access-related issues. Executive and Organizational Leadership Serve on the Stanford Children's Health Senior Leadership Team. Provide clinical leadership for Stanford Health Care Alliance (SHCA) insurance product. Oversee LPCH Case Management functions from Physician-Nurse Dyad perspective and provide UM expertise to that team and Co-Chair Medical Staff Utilization Review Committee Lead Stanford Children's International Patient Services (SCIPS). Serve on SMCH Corporate Compliance Committee Serve on SMCH Committee on Management Controls & Compliance (CMCC) Serve on relevant SMCH committees such as Effectiveness & Efficiency Oversight, Access Steering, Grievance Payer Strategy, Payer Performance, Cell and Gene Therapy Executive Committee Assist with relevant issues discussed at health plan JOC meetings Facilitate the cardiovascular financial clearance review meetings. Be a resource as needed to solid and stem cell transplant as well as gene therapy financial clearance review teams and meetings. Chair the High-Cost Drug Committee and provide guidance on unique high-cost therapies. Qualifications Minimum MD/DO (or equivalent) 5+ years’ experience in physician leadership roles in health plans, hospitals, health care systems or equivalent CA Medical License will be required upon hiring Preferred Qualified to assume 25% clinical role in an academic pediatric and obstetric health system setting Advanced degree, such as MBA, MHA, or equivalent Knowledge Candidate would preferably have experience with: Pediatrics Maternity Epic Accountable Care entities Commercial insurance reimbursement Medicaid insurance reimbursement To Apply Submit CV and a letter of interest with an optional discussion of how your work and experience fosters additional dimensions to the university’s mission and value. Stanford is an equal employment opportunity and affirmative action employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law. Stanford welcomes applications from all who would bring additional dimensions to the University’s research, teaching and clinical missions. Consistent with its obligations under the law, the University will provide reasonable accommodations to applicants and employees with disabilities. Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application or hiring process should contact disability.access@stanford.edu . The university’s central functions of research and education depend on freedom of thought, and expression. The School of Medicine and Stanford University value faculty who will help foster an open and respectful academic environment for colleagues, students, and staff with a wide range of backgrounds, identities, and perspectives. Candidates may choose to include as part of their research and teaching statements a brief discussion about how their work and experience will further these values. For questions, please contact: Dr. Mitchell B. Cohen Elizabeth Wood Dunlevie Professor of Pediatrics Chief Medical Officer, Stanford Medicine Children’s Health Senior Associate Dean for Maternal and Child Health, Stanford Medicine mcohen3@stanford.edu The expected base pay range for this position is $515,000 - $555,000 (at 1.0 FTE). This pay range reflects base pay and an ACMO administrative supplement, which is based on faculty rank and years in rank. It does not include all components of the School of Medicine’s faculty compensation program including pay from participation in departmental incentive compensation programs. For more information about compensation and our wide-range of benefits, including housing assistance, please contact the hiring department. Back to all jobs Apply Now First Name (required) 09c51971 Last Name (required) b95a11e7 Email (required) 94461e68 Refer someone to this job Apply Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Sign up for job alerts Job Alert e53bede3 Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. School School of Medicine School of Medicine Line CE - Clinician Educator CE - Clinician Educator Location Stanford University, California, United States Stanford University, California, United States First Name (required) 4cb7340f Last Name (required) 56287a6b Email (required) 02e7f1d8 Subscribe Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Get Help Questions about a job or your application? Find out how to request help Equal Opportunity Stanford is an equal employment opportunity and affirmative action employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law. Stanford welcomes applications from all who would bring additional dimensions to the University’s research, teaching and clinical missions. Consistent with its obligations under the law, the University will provide reasonable accommodations to applicants and employees with disabilities. Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application or hiring process should contact disability.access@stanford.edu . Stanford University Stanford Home Maps & Directions Search Stanford Emergency Info Terms of Use Cookie Preferences Privacy Copyright Trademarks Non-Discrimination Accessibility © 2026 Stanford University. Stanford, California 94305. Candidate Details We use cookies Some are necessary to operate the website and its functions. Others help personalise, improve content and services to show you the most relevant job opportunities. With the decision \"Accept essential only\" we will respect your privacy and will not set cookies that aren't necessary for the operation of the site. Accept essential only Accept all Please choose the location(s)"
    },
    "https://facultypositions.stanford.edu/jobs/computer-science-assistant-or-untenured-associate-professor-stanford-university-california-united-states-7b5a8eac-558e-4ede-b82f-b25e0f0b801c": {
      "title": "Computer Science Assistant or untenured Associate Professor",
      "description": "Computer Science Assistant or untenured Associate Professor - Stanford University, California, United States Stanford Faculty Positions Site navigation Applicant Login My Profile Kiosk mode Open Faculty Positions Computer Science Assistant or untenured Associate Professor Stanford University University Tenure Line Opening at: Oct 28 2026 - 9:00am PDT Stanford University Department of Computer Science Faculty Opening The Department of Computer Science at Stanford University invites applications for a tenure track faculty position at the junior level (Assistant or untenured Associate Professor). We are seeking applicants from all areas of Computer Science, spanning theoretical foundations, systems, software, and applications. We are also interested in applicants doing research at the frontiers of Computer Science with other disciplines, including but certainly not limited to those in the engineering, mathematical, medical, physical, and social sciences. We give higher priority to the overall originality and promise of the candidate's work than to the candidate’s sub-area of specialization within Computer Science. Further information about the Computer Science Department can be found at https://cs.stanford.edu. The School of Engineering website may be found at https://engineering.stanford.edu . Applicants must have completed, or be completing, a Ph.D. in the advertised field or a closely related one. Evidence of the ability to pursue a program of research, and a strong commitment to graduate and undergraduate teaching are also required. A successful candidate will be expected to teach courses at the graduate and undergraduate levels, and to build and lead a team of graduate students in Ph.D. research. Applications should include a resume, brief statements of research and teaching interests (3-5 pages total for both combined), and the names and e-mail addresses of 3-5 references.  We will request letters of recommendation immediately via the online portal when your application is submitted. Questions should be directed to, Search Committee Chair, c/o Laura Kenny-Carlson, via electronic mail to search@cs.stanford.edu . The Computer Science Department, School of Engineering, and Stanford University value faculty who will help foster an open and respectful academic environment for colleagues, students, and staff with a wide range of backgrounds and perspectives. Candidates may choose to include as part of their research and teaching statements a brief discussion about how their work and experience will further these values. The review of applications will begin on December 1, 2026, and applicants are strongly encouraged to submit complete applications by that date for full consideration; however, applications will continue to be accepted until January 4, 2027. Interviews will begin in the winter term, so applicants are encouraged to apply early. The expected base pay range for this position is: Assistant Professor: $144,100- $163,766 Associate Professor (untenured): $158,510 - $196,803 This base pay range is for a nine-month academic appointment and does not include summer salary.  For more information about compensation and our wide range of benefits, including housing assistance, please contact the hiring department. Stanford University has provided a pay range representing its good faith estimate of what the university reasonably expects to pay for the position upon hire. The pay offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors including (but not limited to) the experience and qualifications of the selected candidate including years since terminal degree, training, and field or discipline; departmental budget availability; internal equity; and external market pay for comparable jobs. Stanford is an equal employment opportunity and affirmative action employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law. Stanford welcomes applications from all who would bring additional dimensions to the University’s research, teaching and clinical missions. Consistent with its obligations under the law, the University will provide reasonableaccommodations to applicants and employees with disabilities. Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application or hiring process should contact disability.access@stanford.edu . Back to all jobs Get Help Questions about a job or your application? Find out how to request help Equal Opportunity Stanford is an equal employment opportunity and affirmative action employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law. Stanford welcomes applications from all who would bring additional dimensions to the University’s research, teaching and clinical missions. Consistent with its obligations under the law, the University will provide reasonable accommodations to applicants and employees with disabilities. Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application or hiring process should contact disability.access@stanford.edu . Stanford University Stanford Home Maps & Directions Search Stanford Emergency Info Terms of Use Cookie Preferences Privacy Copyright Trademarks Non-Discrimination Accessibility © 2026 Stanford University. Stanford, California 94305. Candidate Details We use cookies Some are necessary to operate the website and its functions. Others help personalise, improve content and services to show you the most relevant job opportunities. With the decision \"Accept essential only\" we will respect your privacy and will not set cookies that aren't necessary for the operation of the site. Accept essential only Accept all"
    },
    "https://facultypositions.stanford.edu/jobs/assistant-professor-or-associate-professor-without-tenure-management-science-and-engineering-stanford-university-california-united-states": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor or Associate Professor without Tenure, Management Science and Engineering",
      "description": "Assistant Professor or Associate Professor without Tenure, Management Science and Engineering - Stanford University, California, United States Stanford Faculty Positions Site navigation Applicant Login My Profile Kiosk mode Open Faculty Positions Assistant Professor or Associate Professor without Tenure, Management Science and Engineering Stanford University University Tenure Line Opening at: Aug 18 2026 - 9:00am PDT Closing at: Aug 18 2027 - 11:55pm PDT Stanford University Department of Management Science and Engineering Faculty Opening The Department of Management Science and Engineering at Stanford University invites applications for a tenure track faculty position at the junior level (Assistant or untenured Associate Professor) from individuals working at the frontiers of MS&E. MS&E is a multidisciplinary department with research efforts spanning a broad intellectual spectrum, from analytics and computation to the social and behavioral sciences, united by an interest in engineering solutions to pressing societal problems. Please visit our website for more information about the MS&E Department at http://www.stanford.edu/dept/MSandE/ . Applicants must have completed, or be completing, a Ph.D. in the advertised field or a closely related one. Evidence of the ability to pursue a program of research, and a strong commitment to graduate and undergraduate teaching are also required. A successful candidate will be expected to teach courses at the graduate and undergraduate levels and to build and lead a team of graduate students in PhD research. Applications should include a resume, brief statements of research and teaching interests (3-5 pages total for both combined), and the names and e-mail addresses of 3-5 references. We will request letters of recommendation immediately via the online portal when your application is submitted. If you have questions regarding this opening or require assistance with the online application system, please contact the Faculty Search Administrator at msande-search@stanford.edu . The Department of Management Science & Engineering, School of Engineering, and Stanford University value faculty who will help foster an open and respectful academic environment for colleagues, students, and staff with a wide range of backgrounds and perspectives. Candidates may choose to include as part of their research and teaching statements a brief discussion about how their work and experience will further these values. Review of applications will begin on September 15, 2026; applications submitted by November 1, 2026 will receive full consideration. Interviews will begin in the fall term, so applicants are encouraged to apply early. The expected base pay range for this position is: Assistant Professor: $144,100- $163,766 Associate Professor (untenured): $158,510 - $196,803 This base pay range is for a nine-month academic appointment and does not include summer salary.  For more information about compensation and our wide range of benefits, including housing assistance, please contact the hiring department. Stanford University has provided a pay range representing its good faith estimate of what the university reasonably expects to pay for the position upon hire. The pay offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors including (but not limited to) the experience and qualifications of the selected candidate including years since terminal degree, training, and field or discipline; departmental budget availability; internal equity; and external market pay for comparable jobs. Stanford is an equal employment opportunity and affirmative action employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law. Stanford welcomes applications from all who would bring additional dimensions to the University’s research, teaching and clinical missions. Consistent with its obligations under the law, the University will provide reasonable accommodations to applicants and employees with disabilities. Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application or hiring process should contact disability.access@stanford.edu . Back to all jobs Get Help Questions about a job or your application? Find out how to request help Equal Opportunity Stanford is an equal employment opportunity and affirmative action employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law. Stanford welcomes applications from all who would bring additional dimensions to the University’s research, teaching and clinical missions. Consistent with its obligations under the law, the University will provide reasonable accommodations to applicants and employees with disabilities. Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application or hiring process should contact disability.access@stanford.edu . Stanford University Stanford Home Maps & Directions Search Stanford Emergency Info Terms of Use Cookie Preferences Privacy Copyright Trademarks Non-Discrimination Accessibility © 2026 Stanford University. Stanford, California 94305. Candidate Details We use cookies Some are necessary to operate the website and its functions. Others help personalise, improve content and services to show you the most relevant job opportunities. With the decision \"Accept essential only\" we will respect your privacy and will not set cookies that aren't necessary for the operation of the site. Accept essential only Accept all"
    },
    "https://facultypositions.stanford.edu/jobs/assistant-professor-of-research-pathology-non-tenure-line-research-stanford-university-california-united-states": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Research Pathology (Non-Tenure Line- Research)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Research Pathology (Non-Tenure Line- Research) - Stanford University, California, United States Stanford Faculty Positions Site navigation Applicant Login My Profile Kiosk mode Open Faculty Positions Assistant Professor of Research Pathology (Non-Tenure Line- Research) Stanford University Non-Tenure Line (Research) Opening at: Oct 27 2026 - 12:00pm PDT Closing at: Oct 21 2027 - 11:55pm PDT The Division of Research in the Department of Pathology at Stanford University seeks an outstanding scientist to join the Department as Assistant Professor in the Non-Tenure Line (Research). We are particularly interested in candidates who will maintain an independent research program while also collaborating extensively with clinically active colleagues and trainees in the department. The successful candidate must hold a PhD or equivalent doctoral degree, have extensive experience using cutting-edge research technologies with human samples obtained through anatomic and/or clinical pathology laboratories, demonstrate success in obtaining independent research funding, and a strong commitment to collaborating with clinically active pathology faculty and trainees. The Department of Pathology at Stanford University is one of the leading academic pathology departments in the US with approximately 120 faculty, among whom are a Nobel laureate, HHMI investigators, multiple members of the National Academies, and many faculty with other national distinctions. Stanford Pathology research has areas of focus that include anatomic and clinical pathology, immunology, genetics, cancer research, and neuroscience. We seek an innovative faculty member who will independently and collaboratively inspire and train the next generation of academic pathologists. The major criterion for appointment for faculty in the Non-tenure Line (Research) is evidence of high-level performance as a researcher for whose special knowledge a programmatic need exists. Individuals appointed as Assistant Professors in the Research Line will have completed one or two years of postdoctoral research experience and, where applicable, will have completed housestaff training. Their accomplishments during graduate and postgraduate training should already have stamped them as creative and promising investigators. There should be evidence of the ability to obtain external funding as well as the promise of outstanding performance as a supervisor of graduate students, post-doctoral and clinical fellows, and research scientists. Appointment is based on evidence or the promise of high-level performance in research, and, if applicable, teaching. The initial term of appointment will be four years. This appointment is coterminous with continued salary and other research support from sponsored research projects. Applicants should submit a curriculum vitae, a cover letter, a one-page research statement, a one-page teaching statement, and a one-page list of references to: Faculty Positions Application Link . Applications will be accepted and reviewed until the position is filled. The university’s central functions of research and education depend on freedom of thought, and expression. The Department of Pathology, School of Medicine, and Stanford University value faculty who will help foster an open and respectful academic environment for colleagues, students, and staff with a wide range of backgrounds, identities, and perspectives. Candidates may choose to include as part of their research and teaching statements a brief discussion about how their work and experience will further these values The expected base pay range for this position is: Assistant Professor: $201,000 - $219,000. This pay range reflects base pay, which is based on faculty rank and years in rank. It does not include all components of the School of Medicine’s faculty compensation program or pay from participation in departmental incentive compensation programs. For more information about compensation and our wide-range of benefits , including housing assistance , please contact the hiring department. Stanford University has provided a pay range representing its good faith estimate of what the university reasonably expects to pay for the position upon hire. The pay offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors including (but not limited to) the experience and qualifications of the selected candidate including equivalent years in rank, training, and field or discipline; internal equity; and external market pay for comparable jobs. Stanford is an equal employment opportunity and affirmative action employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law. Stanford welcomes applications from all who would bring additional dimensions to the University’s research, teaching and clinical missions. Consistent with its obligations under the law, the University will provide reasonable accommodations to applicants and employees with disabilities. Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application or hiring process should contact disability.access@stanford.edu . For questions, please contact: Christina Matthews Faculty Affairs Administrator 2 stanfordpathologyfacultyaffairs@stanford.edu Tel: (650) 497-7582 Back to all jobs Get Help Questions about a job or your application? Find out how to request help Equal Opportunity Stanford is an equal employment opportunity and affirmative action employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law. Stanford welcomes applications from all who would bring additional dimensions to the University’s research, teaching and clinical missions. Consistent with its obligations under the law, the University will provide reasonable accommodations to applicants and employees with disabilities. Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application or hiring process should contact disability.access@stanford.edu . Stanford University Stanford Home Maps & Directions Search Stanford Emergency Info Terms of Use Cookie Preferences Privacy Copyright Trademarks Non-Discrimination Accessibility © 2026 Stanford University. Stanford, California 94305. Candidate Details We use cookies Some are necessary to operate the website and its functions. Others help personalise, improve content and services to show you the most relevant job opportunities. With the decision \"Accept essential only\" we will respect your privacy and will not set cookies that aren't necessary for the operation of the site. Accept essential only Accept all"
